section.para:
			Train and save the standard OpenNLP model. Training accepts
			TrainingParameters and an optional DependencyParserFactory
			for additional model artifacts, serializers, and manifest entries:

InputStreamFactory trainingData =
    () -> Files.newInputStream(Path.of("train.conllu"));

TrainingParameters parameters = TrainingParameters.defaultParams();
parameters.put(Parameters.CUTOFF_PARAM, 0);
DependencyModel model;
try (ObjectStream<DependencySample> samples =
    new ConlluDependencySampleStream(trainingData, ConlluTagset.U)) {
  model = DependencyParserME.train("eng", samples, parameters);
}
model.serialize(Path.of("en-depparse.bin"));

DependencyParser parser =
    new DependencyParserME(new DependencyModel(Path.of("en-depparse.bin")));

			The feedforward trainer has its own versioned model format. This example trains,
			saves, reloads, and creates a parser with a beam size of four:

FeedforwardDependencyModel model;
try (ObjectStream<DependencySample> samples =
    new ConlluDependencySampleStream(trainingData, ConlluTagset.U)) {
  model = FeedforwardDependencyTrainer.train(
      samples, FeedforwardDependencyTrainer.Settings.defaults());
}
try (OutputStream out = Files.newOutputStream(Path.of("en-depparse-ff.bin"))) {
  model.serialize(out);
}

DependencyParser parser = new FeedforwardDependencyParser(
    FeedforwardDependencyModel.load(Path.of("en-depparse-ff.bin")), 4);

			Graph indices refer to the input
			token array:

String[] tokens = {"Alice", "sent", "Bob", "a", "message", "."};
String[] tags = {"PROPN", "VERB", "PROPN", "DET", "NOUN", "PUNCT"};
DependencyGraph graph = parser.parse(tokens, tags);

for (DependencyArc arc : graph.arcs()) {
  String dependent = tokens[arc.dependent()];
  String head = arc.head() == DependencyArc.ROOT_HEAD
      ? "ROOT" : tokens[arc.head()];
  System.out.println(dependent + " -> " + head + " (" + arc.relation() + ")");
}

			DependencyEvaluator reports the unlabeled attachment score (the fraction
			of tokens with the correct head) and the labeled attachment score (the fraction with
			the correct head and relation label), both over all tokens and, through the
			ExcludingPunctuation getters, over the tokens whose gold tag is not
			PUNCT. DependencyCrossValidator splits a sample stream into
			folds, trains a parser on the other folds through a trainer you supply, and scores
			the held-out fold:

DependencyCrossValidator validator = new DependencyCrossValidator(
    samples -> new DependencyParserME(
        DependencyParserME.train("eng", samples, parameters)));
validator.evaluate(trainingSamples, 4);
double uas = validator.getUas();
double lasExcludingPunctuation = validator.getLasExcludingPunctuation();

			The provided treebank evaluation, UniversalDependencyParserEval in
			opennlp-eval-tests, trains the parser from scratch on a Universal
			Dependencies 2.0 training split and pins all four scores on data the parser has not
			seen: the transition parser on the English, German, Spanish AnCora, and French
			development splits and in a five-fold cross validation of the English training split.
			It uses
			gold sentence boundaries, tokens, and tags, so it measures the dependency parser
			rather than a complete text pipeline. The arc-standard trainer skips non-projective
			training samples. Evaluation uses
			the existing opennlp-data treebanks.
